{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793552972,"nonce":"Li58OvzDjQplsk/jYSVGMvFwyd8=","title":"Cloud Sync: write browser edits back to the cloud copy","message":"A synced workspace opens in the browser as a restore of the newest generation. Edits made there live only in the session's working tree: the next Open (or a gateway or runner restart) restores afresh and discards them, and the desktop never sees them. Today the browser is a viewer of a snapshot, not a workspace.\n\nWhat a write-back needs:\n- A way for the session's tree to become a new generation. The publish protocol already exists (lease, chunk upload, compare-and-swap publish against a base generation), so a write-back is a publish with base = the generation that was restored. A concurrent desktop push then fails the compare-and-swap and has to surface as a conflict, not overwrite.\n- A credential. Publishing is account-authenticated and the session host (a sandbox, or the local dev stand-in) has no account. Options: (a) the browser page, which is signed in, asks the session for a scan and uploads the chunks itself; (b) the Worker mints a short-lived publish capability bound to the workspace, owner and base generation when it opens the workspace, and gives it to the runner/session; (c) the runner publishes on the account's behalf through a runner-authenticated route scoped to the workspace's owner.\n- A trigger: explicit Save to cloud, on session close, or periodic. Unsaved editor buffers are separate (the manifest has a buffers section).\n- What is included: working-tree files as the desktop scan does; whether history comes back is open.\n- The scan used on the desktop shells out to git (see ab77805); the session host needs the same selection rules.\n\nDecisions needed before building: credential model (a/b/c above), trigger, conflict policy.\n\nRelated: e003324, 529764c, 4f39ade.","files":null}]}